Now let me understand the vulnerability. In `ndpi_search_setup_capwap`:

```c
if(((sport == NDPI_CAPWAP_CONTROL_PORT) || (dport == NDPI_CAPWAP_CONTROL_PORT))
   && ((packet->payload[0] == 0x0) || (packet->payload[0] == 0x1))
   ) {
  u_int16_t msg_len, offset, to_add;

  if(packet->payload[0] == 0x0)
    offset = 13, to_add = 13;
  else
    offset = 15, to_add = 17;

  msg_len = ntohs(*(u_int16_t*)&packet->payload[offset]);

  if((msg_len+to_add) == packet->payload_packet_len)
    goto capwap_found;
}
```

Note: This second branch is guarded by `sport` or `dport` == control port and `payload[0]` being 0 or 1, but NOT by `payload_packet_len >= 16`! So if the payload is shorter than offset+2 (i.e., less than 15 bytes when offset=13), the read `*(u_int16_t*)&packet->payload[offset]` reads beyond the payload buffer. This is a buffer overread.

Also the first branch does a check `payload_packet_len >= 16` but is evaluated with `&&` and also reads payload[8] before... no, with && the payload_packet_len check comes before, so payload[8] only evaluated if length >= 16. Fine.

The second block: `((sport == NDPI_CAPWAP_CONTROL_PORT) || (dport == NDPI_CAPWAP_CONTROL_PORT)) && ((packet->payload[0] == 0x0) || (packet->payload[0] == 0x1))`. If payload_packet_len is e.g. 1 byte, and payload[0]==0, then offset=13, and reading payload[13..14] reads beyond. Overread.

So to trigger: a UDP packet to or from port 5246 with payload length >= 1 byte but < 15 bytes (offset 13 + 2 = 15), with payload[0] == 0.

Actually, there's a protocol selection mask: `NDPI_SELECTION_BITMASK_PROTOCOL_UDP_WITH_PAYLOAD` with `SAVE_DETECTION_BITMASK_AS_UNKNOWN`. So a UDP packet with nonzero payload. If payload length is 1 with payload[0]=0... wait, but can payload_packet_len be 1? Yes, a UDP packet with 1 byte of payload.

Hmm, but wait — does the packet make it to this dissector? CAPWAP is only checked when protocol unknown and UDP. The payload needs >= 1 byte (UDP_WITH_PAYLOAD). So a 1-byte UDP payload with byte value 0 to port 5246 would cause overread reading payload[13], payload[14].

